---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Why is my Telenor internet slow?',
    a: 'The usual reasons, in order: your bundle has run out, weak signal where you are, the phone is on 3G instead of 4G, a wrong APN, or peak-hour tower congestion (7–11 pm). Telenor also has the lowest 4G averages of Pakistan’s big four networks on the PTA’s Q2 2026 benchmarking (7.5 Mbps download), so expectations should be lower than on Zong or Jazz — but under 2 Mbps in a good-signal area still means something is wrong.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is a good Telenor 4G speed?',
    a: 'The PTA’s Q2 2026 benchmarking puts Telenor’s national 4G average at 7.5 Mbps download and 2.8 Mbps upload — the lowest of the big four. That’s enough for browsing, messaging and HD video, but tight for 4K or heavy work calls. If you consistently get under 2 Mbps in a good-signal area, work through the steps in this guide.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Telenor still working after the Ufone merger?',
    a: 'Yes. PTCL bought Telenor Pakistan in December 2025 and merged it into Ufone on 1 July 2026, but Telenor SIMs still work. If the old Telenor settings stop connecting, try the Ufone settings instead — APN ufone.pinternet for prepaid or ufone.internet for postpaid.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I complain about slow Telenor internet?',
    a: 'Call Telenor on 345 from your Telenor number. Before you call, run three speed tests at different times and note the results, your location, and whether the problem is everywhere or in one spot — that evidence gets a faster, better answer than "my internet is slow".',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Guides', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/guides' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Telenor Internet Slow?', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/guides/telenor-internet-slow' },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Telenor Internet Slow? How to Fix Slow Telenor 4G Speed (2026)"
  description="Telenor internet slow? Fix it in order: bundle check, signal, 4G-only mode, APN settings, old SIM — and what the Ufone merger means for your Telenor SIM."
  canonical="/guides/telenor-internet-slow"
  schema={[faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/guides">Guides</a> / Telenor Internet Slow</p>
      <h1>Telenor Internet Slow? How to Fix Slow Telenor 4G</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Telenor has the lowest 4G averages of Pakistan’s big four networks — so slow
        Telenor isn’t always a fault. But if your speed is well below even Telenor’s
        modest average, work through these fixes in order.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 720px;">
      <h2>Step 1: Check your bundle is active</h2>
      <p>
        The most common cause of slow mobile internet is the simplest: the data bundle
        has run out or expired. Dial <strong>*123#</strong> to check your remaining
        balance and bundle status. No bundle, no data — or data at a crawl on
        pay-as-you-go rates.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 2: Check your signal</h2>
      <p>
        Mobile speed is mostly signal. Move near a window or outdoors and test again —
        one or two bars indoors can become four bars outside. If the speed jumps, the
        problem was your location, not the network. Walls, basements and metal roofs all
        eat signal.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 3: Make sure you’re on 4G</h2>
      <p>
        If the status bar shows E, H or H+, you’re on 2G/3G — far slower than 4G. Go to
        Settings → Mobile Network → Preferred Network Type and set it to 4G or LTE. On
        dual-SIM phones, check the Telenor SIM is in the slot that supports 4G.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 4: Toggle airplane mode</h2>
      <p>
        Switch airplane mode on for 30 seconds, then off. This forces the phone to
        re-register with the nearest tower, which fixes the common case where the phone
        is clinging to a distant, overloaded tower.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 5: Check the APN</h2>
      <p>
        The APN (Access Point Name) tells your phone how to reach Telenor’s data
        network. It should read <strong>internet</strong>. The easiest fix is to get the
        settings sent to your phone: send <strong>"Internet"</strong> to
        <strong>131</strong>, then install the message when it arrives. See our full
        <a href="/guides/telenor-apn-settings"> Telenor APN settings guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 6: Check the SIM</h2>
      <p>
        An old 3G-era SIM can’t run 4G properly. If the SIM is years old, swap it for a
        4G SIM at a Telenor franchise (biometric verification needed).
      </p>

      <h2>Step 7: Test at a quiet time</h2>
      <p>
        Evenings (7–11 pm) are the busiest hours on any mobile network. Test early in the
        morning and again at 9 pm: if mornings are fast and evenings are slow, that’s
        tower congestion, and no phone setting will fix it. Keep both results as evidence.
      </p>

      <h2>What speed should you expect?</h2>
      <p>
        The PTA’s Q2 2026 benchmarking puts Telenor’s national 4G average at
        <strong>7.5 Mbps download and 2.8 Mbps upload</strong> — the lowest of
        Pakistan’s big four networks (Zong leads at 18.6 Mbps). That’s enough for
        browsing, messaging and HD video, but tight for 4K or heavy work calls. If you
        consistently get under 2 Mbps in a good-signal area at a quiet time, something
        is wrong.
      </p>

      <h2>A note on the Ufone merger</h2>
      <p>
        PTCL bought Telenor Pakistan in December 2025, and on 1 July 2026 Telenor
        Pakistan was merged into Ufone. Telenor SIMs still work — but if the old Telenor
        settings stop connecting, try the Ufone settings instead: APN
        <strong>ufone.pinternet</strong> for prepaid or <strong>ufone.internet</strong>
        for postpaid. See our <a href="/guides/ufone-apn-settings">Ufone APN
        settings guide</a> for the full values.
      </p>

      <h2>Still slow? Complain with evidence</h2>
      <p>
        Call Telenor on <strong>345</strong> from your Telenor number. Before you call,
        run three speed tests at different times, note the results, your location, and
        whether the problem is everywhere or in one spot. That evidence gets a faster,
        better answer than "my internet is slow".
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details class="faq">
            <summary>{f.q}</summary>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="cta-band">
        <h2>Test your Telenor speed</h2>
        <p>Run a test now and compare with the PTA averages above.</p>
        <div class="cta-links">
          <a class="btn" href="/speedtest/telenor">Telenor Speed Test →</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/telenor-apn-settings">Telenor APN settings →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
